METHOD: Real browser (Playwright/chromium 1.63.0-alpha, from the cached
@playwright/mcp npx install; axe-core 4.13 from a cached npx install), not
template reading. Built the worktree (`go build ./cmd/server`) and ran the
binary against an isolated scratch data dir outside the repo
(BUDGET_DATA_DIR=/tmp/.../scratchpad/s4-data, BUDGET_LISTEN_ADDR=:18453).
Created real overlapping accounts and real *.csv files on disk through the
actual "Add an account" / "Save changes" forms (same code path a user
hits: a-wide pattern "usaa", b-narrow pattern "usaa-checking", both
matching usaa-checking-2026.csv). All dismiss activations were via
page.keyboard.press('Enter') after Tab-walking from document start or
programmatic .focus() on the button node — never .click(). Repo `git
status` confirmed clean before and after; server killed at the end.

POINT 10 (announcement) — PASS.
Tab-walked from document.body (46 hops, real sequential navigation, no
skips) and landed on the button carrying data-dismiss-warnings. Armed a
MutationObserver on #accounts-warnings-region before activating. Pressing
Enter produced exactly 1 mutation; region.textContent went from the full
warning text to "Account overlap warning dismissed." — a real, distinct
confirmation, not a repeat of the warning and not silence. The region is
the same node registered since before the dismissal (role="status"
aria-live="polite", confirmed via getAttribute both before and after), so
a screen reader that already has it registered receives the new text.
Re-verified region node-identity separately: tagged with a probe attribute
before two unrelated HTMX swaps, `list.contains(region) === false` and the
node survives both swaps unchanged (attempt-2 finding still holds — this
diff did not touch that mechanism). Visible banner carries no
role/aria-live of its own (both null), so exactly one thing announces.

POINT 10 (focus) — PASS.
document.activeElement after Enter is exactly #accounts-new-heading ("Add
an account"), not <body> — attempt 2's failure does not reproduce.
document-order check: the warnings banner (#accounts-warnings-banner) is
immediately followed in the DOM by the "Add an account" <section
aria-labelledby="accounts-new-heading">; #accounts-new-heading is the
literal next heading after the banner. This is a defensible "sensible
element inside the swapped region" under the constitution's wording — it
is the next landmark-section heading in reading order, the same pattern
already used by the pre-existing #accounts-list-heading fallback for
regular validation-error swaps, and it does not skip the user past
unvisited content the way jumping to the page's own <h1> would. No trap:
pressing Tab again from the heading moves forward to #acct-new-id (the
first field of the very next section), a sensible continuation, not stuck
and not looping back into the now-removed banner.

TABINDEX="-1" RESIDUE — PASS, no misleading side effects found.
Immediately after dismiss, #accounts-new-heading carries tabindex="-1" (as
designed, so .focus() can target a non-interactive heading). Confirmed
this does NOT pull the heading into the page's normal sequential Tab
order: on a fresh reload, 50 Tab presses from document start never focus
#accounts-new-heading (tabindex="-1" correctly excludes it from
sequential navigation; it is reachable only programmatically). Confirmed
it does not leak into or get overwritten by the *other*, pre-existing
fallback path: triggering a normal validation error on the create form
(duplicate account ID, a real server-side rejection that re-swaps
#accounts-list) produces a brand-new #accounts-new-heading DOM node with
tabindex === null — the server never renders this attribute, only the
dismiss handler's client-side JS sets it, and only on the node live at
dismiss time. It does not persist across subsequent unrelated swaps in a
way that changes their focus order, because each swap replaces the whole
#accounts-list subtree (including this heading) with a fresh node from
the server. This mirrors the already-existing htmx:afterSwap fallback for
#accounts-list-heading (same tabindex="-1"-and-never-removed pattern,
present in the tree before this diff) — consistent with established
practice in this codebase, not a new kind of residue.

SAME-TEXT GUARD, both directions, AFTER a dismissal — PASS, all three
sub-cases reproduced exactly as specified via MutationObserver:
  (a) Unrelated mutation after dismissal (edited b-narrow's
      low_balance_threshold, warnings content unchanged): 0 further
